[38;2;124;124;124mBy Mertens' theorem, $\sum_{p \leq x} \frac{\log p}{p} \approx \log x$. So the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribution is approximately $n \log \log n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMeanwhile, $\log n! \approx n \log n$. So the small primes (up to $\log n$) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribute only about $n \log \log n$ to $\log n!$, which is much less than $n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\log n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo making $n$ a primorial only "saves" about $n \log \log n$ out of $n \log n$, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is not enough. We need to save almost all of $\log n!$ (all but $0.001 n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\log n$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHmm, so the primorial approach doesn't directly work. We need a different [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstrategy.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me reconsider. The key fact is: $p | n \implies p \nmid N_n$. So if $n$ is [0m
