[38;2;124;124;124mprimes" — primes that can divide N(n) for some n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me understand the density of good primes. Up to 500, there are 36 good [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprimes out of 95 primes total. That's about 38%.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me check the density further and also understand the structure of bad primes[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbetter.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about the problem more carefully. The key question is: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcan we find n such that g(n) > n^{0.999n}?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mg(n) = n! / gcd(N(n), n!). We need gcd(N(n), n!) to be small.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe primes that can divide gcd(N(n), n!) are exactly the "bad primes" p ≤ n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(since good primes never divide N(n)). For bad primes p, p | N(n) iff n mod p ∈ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mB_p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo gcd(N(n), n!) = product over bad primes p ≤ n with n mod p ∈ B_p of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mp^{min(v_p(N(n)), v_p(n!))}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe contribution of each such prime p to log(gcd) is min(v_p(N(n)), v_p(n!)) * [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlog p ≤ v_p(n!) * log p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the gcd to be small enough, we need the total contribution to be less than [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlog(n!) - 0.999 n log n ≈ 0.001 n log n - n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe main threat is small bad primes. For a small bad prime p (like 2, 5, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m13, ...), v_p(n!) ~ n/(p-1) which is large. So if p | N(n), the contribution is [0m[38;2;124;124;124m~ n/(p-1) * log p.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor p = 2: if n is odd, 2 | N(n), and v_2(n!) ~ n, so contribution ~ n log 2 ~ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0.69n. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mFor p = 5: if n ≡ 2 or 4 mod 5, 5 | N(n), and v_5(n!) ~ n/4, so contribution ~ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(n/4) log 5 ~ 0.40n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo even a single small bad prime dividing N(n) can contribute O(n) to log(gcd), [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhich is comparable to our budget of 0.001 n log n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor n ~ e^{1000}, 0.001 n log n = n, so the budget is ~n. A single bad prime [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlike 2 contributes ~0.69n, and 5 contributes ~0.40n. So if both 2 and 5 divide [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mN(n), that's already ~1.09n > n, exceeding the budget.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need to carefully choose n to avoid bad primes, especially small ones.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut the budget grows: for n = e^{2000}, the budget is 0.001 * 2000 * n - n = n. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mWait, 0.001 n log n - n = n(0.001 * 2000 - 1) = n(2 - 1) = n. For n = e^{3000}, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbudget = n(3-1) = 2n. For n = e^{10000}, budget = n(10-1) = 9n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo for very large n, the budget is (0.001 log n - 1) * n, which grows. But the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribution from small bad primes is at most sum over bad primes p | N(n) of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(n/(p-1)) log p. The worst case is if all small bad primes divide N(n), giving [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribution ~ n * sum_{p bad} log p / (p-1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me compute sum over bad primes of log p / (p-1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the sum over ALL primes of log p / (p-1) diverges (it's ~ log n by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mPNT). But we only sum over bad primes that actually divide N(n), and we can try [0m[38;2;124;124;124mto choose n to avoid many of them.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this differently. The total contribution to log(gcd) from all[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbad primes p ≤ n is at most:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124msum_{p ≤ n, p bad, n mod p ∈ B_p} v_p(n!) * log p[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m≤ sum_{p ≤ n, p bad, n mod p ∈ B_p} (n/(p-1)) * log p[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m= n * sum_{p ≤ n, p bad, n mod p ∈ B_p} log p / (p-1)[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need this to be < 0.001 n log n - n, i.e.,[0m

[38;2;124;124;124msum_{p ≤ n, p bad, n mod p ∈ B_p} log p / (p-1) < 0.001 log n - 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, sum over all bad primes p ≤ n of log p / (p-1) ~ (fraction of bad primes) *[0m[38;2;124;124;124mlog n. If about 60% of primes are bad, this is ~ 0.6 log n, which is way more [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthan 0.001 log n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we can't afford to have all bad primes dividing N(n). We need to avoid most [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof them.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe fraction of bad primes p that actually divide N(n) (for a "random" n) is |[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mB_p|/p. If |B_p| is typically small (like 1 or 2), then the expected [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontribution is:[0m

